\ Bounded-memory regression for a helper defined inside a word body that calls an `inline` word.
\
\ The words below are defined before `add-one`, so their helpers still hold plain calls to it when
\ each call into the word defines them. Expansion built a new body for the helper on every
\ definition and listed it for teardown, which exhausted the cap in the `.flags` sidecar. A body
\ defined again now reuses the expansion of its first definition, so every loop stays flat.

single: ( n -- n ) [
  helper: ( n -- n ) [ add-one { 1 2 } drop add-one ] ;
  helper
] ;

nested: ( n -- n ) [
  helper: ( n -- n ) [ [ add-one { 1 2 } drop ] call add-one ] ;
  helper
] ;

captures: ( n -- n ) [
  x: swap ;
  helper: ( n -- n ) [ add-one x + ] ;
  1 helper
] ;

add-one: ( n -- n ) const inline [ 1 + ] ;

40000 [ 1 single drop ] times
40000 [ 1 nested drop ] times
3000 [ 0 captures drop ] times

"flat" print-line
